## Develop

Requirements: rustup, Python 3.11+, just, Godot 4.7+, and a native compiler/linker.
Rust is pinned to 1.97.1 and godot-rust to 0.5.5. Set `GODOT` to your engine
executable if `godot` is not on PATH. Windows recipes use `python`; other hosts
use `python3`.

```sh
just demo          # Build release runtime + debug editor and run the sandbox
just editor        # Build and open the sandbox in Godot
just check         # rustfmt and Clippy for runtime and editor configurations
just check-godot   # Build both libraries and import the sandbox headlessly
just check-all     # All of the above checks
just build         # Build optimized runtime and editor libraries
just package       # Build and package the current host's addon in dist/
```

`[[[ class_prefix ]]]Node` is available in both runtime and editor builds. It
exports `message` in the Inspector and exposes `greet()` to GDScript. Extend
`rust/src/runtime/` with your native functionality. The `editor` Cargo feature
adds `[[[ class_prefix ]]]EditorPlugin` and class documentation; extend
`rust/src/editor/` with editor tools. The native editor plugin is registered
automatically and does not need a GDScript `plugin.cfg`.

The demo lives in `godot/demo/`; the sandbox uses Compatibility rendering so the
minimal example does not require Vulkan. The import check catches engine errors
and verifies that the editor library loads. No test suites are included.

Build helpers atomically publish libraries into `addon/bin/` and copy the addon
into ignored `godot/addons/[[[ addon_name ]]]/`. Edit `addon/`, not that generated
copy. Run one build/publish operation at a time. Close Godot and retry if Windows
locks a loaded DLL.

Both Godot debug and release exports use the optimized runtime library. Editor
libraries are selected separately; optimized editor builds retain debug assertions
for gdext's Linux hot-reload support. Packaged descriptors disable hot reload.

## Install and package

After `just build`, copy `addon/` to `addons/[[[ addon_name ]]]/` in your Godot
project. Add a `[[[ class_prefix ]]]Node` to a scene and call `greet()`.
Alternatively, extract `dist/[[[ addon_name ]]]-<version>-<target>.zip` into the
project. `rust/Cargo.toml` supplies the addon version; the descriptor's
`compatibility_minimum` specifies the minimum Godot version.

Build/package helpers accept `--target <target>`; IDs are listed in
`scripts/release_targets.py`. Desktop targets need runtime and editor libraries;
Android, iOS, and Web need runtime only. For example:

```sh
python3 scripts/native_build.py build runtime --release --target aarch64-linux-android
python3 scripts/native_build.py package --target aarch64-linux-android
```

Install the Rust target and any cross compiler before building. Android needs
`ANDROID_NDK_HOME` (the release workflow pins NDK 29.0.14206865). iOS needs macOS,
Xcode, and the `aarch64-apple-ios`, `aarch64-apple-ios-sim`, and
`x86_64-apple-ios` Rust targets. Its `ios-universal` package contains device and
simulator XCFramework slices with bundle identifier prefix
`[[[ ios_bundle_identifier_prefix ]]]`; sign frameworks with the exported app and
use an iOS deployment target of at least 16.0.

Web needs Emscripten 4.0.20 and Rust `nightly-2026-01-22` with `rust-src` and the
`wasm32-unknown-emscripten` target. Web support is experimental; enable Extensions
Support in Godot's export preset. Threaded exports require cross-origin isolation
headers. Keep Rust, bindings, engine API, descriptor minimum, and CI engine pins
aligned when upgrading.

Local library paths are shared across architectures: package each target before
building another, then rebuild the native host before reopening the sandbox.
The release merger puts each target in its own directory in the combined archive.
Validate platform exports on their intended devices.
