## GitHub releases

CI runs formatting, lint, headless import, and packaging on hosted Linux, Windows,
and macOS runners. The Release workflow runs release-please on `main`. Conventional
`feat:` and `fix:` commits create a release PR; the first release is `v0.1.0`.
Merging it builds all 16 release targets and uploads one
`[[[ addon_name ]]]-<version>.zip`. The changelog is created by release-please.

Install a GitHub App on the repository with read/write permissions for contents,
issues, and pull requests. Configure Actions secrets `RELEASE_APP_CLIENT_ID`
and `RELEASE_APP_PRIVATE_KEY` with its client ID and PEM private key. App tokens
allow checks to run on release PRs; packaging/upload uses `GITHUB_TOKEN` with
job-specific permissions.

To rebuild an existing release, dispatch the Release workflow from `main` with
`tag` set to its tag (for example `v0.1.0`). The existing combined asset is replaced
after all target packages pass validation. Release-please's changelog-only `go`
strategy updates the Rust manifest and lockfile through explicit TOML entries.
